    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# US vs non-US share\n",
    "us_count = df_2010['country'].str.contains('United States', na=False).sum()\n",
    "total_with_country = df_2010['country'].notna().sum()\n",
    "print(f\"\\nTitles with US involvement: {us_count:,} / {total_with_country:,} ({us_count / total_with_country * 100:.1f}%)\")\n",
    "print(f\"Non-US titles: {total_with_country - us_count:,} ({(total_with_country - us_count) / total_with_country * 100:.1f}%)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Genre Deep-Dive: What Kind of Series Is Netflix Betting On?\n",
    "\n",
    "The previous analysis established that Netflix is pivoting from a movie-heavy to a series-first model. But \"make more series\" is not a strategy — it's a direction. A content acquisition team needs to know: **which genres are growing fastest, and what does that reveal about Netflix's underlying bet on viewer behavior?**\n",
    "\n",
    "If the pivot is uniform across all genres, the recommendation is simple: scale everything. If it's concentrated in specific genres, that tells us something about what Netflix believes drives retention."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Total genre tags: 16,322\n",
      "Unique genres: 42\n",
      "\n",
      "Top 10 Genres by Volume — Growth since 2010:\n",
      "                   Genre  Growth_Multiplier\n",
      "               TV Dramas           6.384615\n",
      "             TV Comedies           6.171429\n",
      "           Documentaries           6.115385\n",
      "  International TV Shows           5.518519\n",
      "    International Movies           2.014286\n",
      "Children & Family Movies           1.846154\n",
      "                  Dramas           1.437500\n",
      "      Action & Adventure           1.321429\n",
      "                Comedies           1.280488\n",
      "      Independent Movies           0.941176\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Parse multi-genre tags into individual genres\n",
    "df_genre = df_2010[df_2010['listed_in'].notna()].copy()\n",
    "df_genre['genre'] = df_genre['listed_in'].str.split(', ')\n",
    "df_exploded = df_genre.explode('genre')\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Total genre tags: {len(df_exploded):,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Unique genres: {df_exploded['genre'].nunique()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- Analysis A: Top 10 genres by total volume ---\n",
    "top10 = df_exploded['genre'].value_counts().head(10).index\n",
    "df_top10 = df_exploded[df_exploded['genre'].isin(top10)].copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "genre_counts = df_top10.groupby(['release_year', 'genre']).size().unstack(fill_value=0)\n",
    "base_period = genre_counts.loc[2010:2012].mean()\n",
    "genre_growth = genre_counts.div(base_period, axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "summary = genre_growth.loc[2021].reset_index()\n",
    "summary.columns = ['Genre', 'Growth_Multiplier']\n",
    "summary = summary.sort_values(by='Growth_Multiplier', ascending=False).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "print(\"\\nTop 10 Genres by Volume — Growth since 2010:\")\n",
    "print(summary.to_string(index=False))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x700 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Plot: Volume leaders growth over time\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 7))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Highlight top 3 and bottom 1, grey out the rest\n",
    "highlight = ['TV Dramas', 'TV Comedies', 'Documentaries', 'Independent Movies']\n",
    "colors = {'TV Dramas': '#e50914', 'TV Comedies': '#b81d24', 'Documentaries': '#221f1f', 'Independent Movies': '#999999'}\n",
    "\n",
    "for genre in genre_growth.columns:\n",
    "    if genre in highlight:\n",
    "        ax.plot(genre_growth.index, genre_growth[genre], marker='o',\n",
    "                linewidth=2.5, label=genre, color=colors[genre])\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        ax.plot(genre_growth.index, genre_growth[genre],\n",
    "                linewidth=1, alpha=0.3, color='#cccccc')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.axhline(y=1, color='grey', linestyle='--', alpha=0.5, label='Baseline (2010)')\n",
    "ax.set_title('Growth of Top 10 Genres by Volume (Normalized to 2010)', fontsize=15, fontweight='bold', pad=15)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Release Year', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Growth Multiplier (1.0 = 2010 level)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax.legend(loc='upper left', fontsize=10)\n",
    "ax.set_xticks(genre_growth.index)\n",
    "ax.tick_params(axis='x', rotation=45)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Finding:** Among Netflix's largest genres, TV Dramas (6.4x), TV Comedies (6.2x), and Documentaries (6.1x) lead in growth — all three are series-friendly formats. Meanwhile, Independent Movies is the only genre with a growth multiplier below 1 (0.94), meaning Netflix is actively producing *fewer* independent films than it did in 2010.\n",
    "\n",
    "This confirms the series-first pivot at the genre level: Netflix is not just shifting from movies to TV shows in aggregate — it's specifically abandoning long-tail, niche movie formats (independent film) and reallocating those slots to high-engagement series genres.\n",
